{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/636699-a-muslim-covenant","id":636699,"title":"A Muslim Covenant","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-08-22T23:13:18-07:00","id":636699,"title":"A Muslim Covenant","content":"<span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">However one interprets the Lancea et Sanctum, the fact remains that it\u2019s grounded in a distinctively Christian theology. Given that Islam is the second largest religion in the world, it seems appropriate for there to be a covenant that\u2019s equally grounded in (and equally distinct from) Islam. Here\u2019s my attempt at putting together something like that; it's pretty rough, but let me know what you think!</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">(Note: I haven\u2019t come up with an appropriate name for the covenant; where it would appear in the text, I\u2019ve currently got a bunch of XXXs)</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">A Little Background</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Kindred of what would become the Muslim world were, on the whole, slower to adopt Islam than were their mortal counterparts. Before the establishment of the Caliphate, the most significant faith among the vampires of the region was Longinian Christianity, which was prevalent throughout the Eastern Roman Empire, particularly in Egypt and the Greek cultural sphere. In addition, many vampires in Mesopotamia continued to practice truly ancient rites in honor of deities all but forgotten by the living, and throughout the region scattered sects of blood-drenched celebrants embraced deviant creeds that rejected all human morality.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">As Islam gained ground among the cities of north Africa and southwest Asia, however, more and more members of the new religion were embraced. Those neonates who held firm to their faith were driven to the edge of despair, however, for the mockery of life into which they had been thrust seemed incompatible with the strictures of their religion. Relief came during the 9th century, in the form of the </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>\u1e62a\u1e25\u012b\u1e25 Ibn al-D\u016bda</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>\u1e62a\u1e25\u012b\u1e25 Ibn al-D\u016bda</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> is a short collection of </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>\u02bea\u1e25\u0101d\u012bth</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> attributed to an eponymous kindred known only as Ibn al-D\u016bda, the \u201cSon of the Worm.\u201d It is, to say the least, an unconventional example of its genre, particularly in that the author of the text claims to have been embraced some time before Muhammad experienced his first revelation, and that what he has recorded consists solely of his own first-hand experiences in the company of the Prophet. Mortal Muslims of any major sect would almost certainly reject the </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>\u1e25\u0101d\u012bth </i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">of the </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>\u1e62a\u1e25\u012b\u1e25 Ibn al-D\u016bda</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> as </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>maw\u1e0d\u016b\u02bb</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, fabrications, but among the Muslim kindred of the </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">XXX</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> it is held in the highest regard.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>\u1e62a\u1e25\u012b\u1e25 Ibn al-D\u016bda</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> reports that Muhammad preached not only to the living, but to the kindred as well. As it is the will of Allah that vampires remain hidden from mortals, Muhammad never revealed his dealings to the main body of the kindred. The vampires of the Prophet\u2019s time were proud and stubborn, and of them only Ibn al-D\u016bda paid his words any heed, dutifully recording them in preparation for the day when his kindred would be ready to accept their truth.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b>Ark\u0101n al-D\u012bn: The Pillars of the Faith</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>\u1e62a\u1e25\u012b\u1e25 Ibn al-D\u016bda</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> affirms that Allah has prescribed a </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>shar\u012b\u02bfah</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> for the kindred, just as he has for all of the other creatures in His creation. Just as the kindred resemble mortal human beings in many ways, the </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>shar\u012b\u02bfah</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> of the kindred bears many similarities to the </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>shar\u012b\u02bfah</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> of living Muslims. That said, the truth of the matter is that the kindred are not human, and the duties that Allah has set before them are different from those which He has given to humanity.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>Shah\u0101da: Testimony</i></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>L\u0101 \u02beil\u0101ha \u02beill\u0101-ll\u0101hu mu\u1e25ammadun ras\u016blu-ll\u0101h - </i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">\u201cthere is no god but God, and Muhammad is the messenger of God.\u201d This statement summarizes the core of the Muslim faith, and It is essential for both mortals and kindred to utter it in order to become Muslims and to practice Islam.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>\u1e62al\u0101h: Prayer</i></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Because Allah has decreed that the kindred shall rise only at night, and because a public call to prayer would run counter to the secrecy of the kindred, Allah has prescribed a somewhat reduced schedule of prayers for the kindred. Kindred are expected to perform the </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>\u1e63al\u0101t al-ma\u0121rib</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, the evening prayer, upon arising for the night, and the </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>\u1e63al\u0101t al-fajr</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, the dawn prayer, before return to daysleep. The </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>\u1e63al\u0101t al-\u02bfi\u0161\u0101\u02be</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, the nighttime prayer, is ideally performed after the vampire first feeds during the night, but may otherwise take place any time between the other two prayers.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The kindred are expected to pray in the same manner as their mortal counterparts, and must likewise perform </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>al-wu\u1e0d\u016b\u02bc</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, ablution, before each prayer. However, Allah has decreed that the living and the dead shall not worship together, and the kindred are forbidden from praying at mortal mosques. The vampires of some domains have constructed their own, hidden mosques for their nightly prayers, but most of the </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">XXX</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> simply pray wherever it is convenient.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>Zak\u0101h: Charity</i></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Among mortal Muslims, </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>zak\u0101h</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, literally \u201cthat which purifies,\u201d refers to the practice of alms-giving, which is considered a way to purify one's income and wealth from sometimes worldly, impure way of acquisition. While vampires who continue to accumulate wealth after the embrace are required to continue paying </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>zak\u0101h</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> as they did when mortal, vampires who control territory and herds are expected to provide for their fellow kindred as well. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">In all domains where the </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">XXX </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">have influence, an area is set aside for the use of the \u201cpoor and needy\u201d - those who have no other means of feeding themselves. In some domains, vampires with extensive herds may even make some of their vessels available to support those in need. These resources are generally only available to muslim vampires, and sometimes only to those who have sworn allegiance to the covenant. In addition to being a divinely mandated moral duty, </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>zak\u0101h</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> has the added benefit of reducing the number of hungry vampires in the domain, which reduces the incidence of frenzy and thereby helps preserve the secrecy of the kindred.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>\u1e62awm: Abstaining</i></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Among mortal Muslims, </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>\u1e62awm</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, meaning \u201cto abstain,\u201d refers to fasting, particularly during the holy month of Ramadan. Among the vampires of the </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">XXX</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, it refers specifically to an injunction against feeding on one\u2019s kindred. In particular, the </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>\u1e62a\u1e25\u012b\u1e25 Ibn al-D\u016bda</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> condemns the practice of diablerie, but many Muslim kindred believe that this prohibition extends to forced blood bonds and \u201cperversion\u201d as well.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>\u1e24ajj: Pilgrimage</i></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">It is not the will of Allah that kindred should travel far from the places of their embrace, save under the most dire of circumstances. This is one reason why he has made them vulnerable to sunlight, so that it is difficult for them to travel anywhere that cannot be reached in a single night. Thus, the kindred are not required to engage in a physical pilgrimage to Mecca, as mortal Muslims are.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>\u1e62a\u1e25\u012b\u1e25 Ibn al-D\u016bda</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> uses the term </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>Hajj</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> in an allegorical sense, to refer to the often lonely journey of the kindred through an eternity of night. Much as the Druze interpret </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>Hajj</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> to refer to &quot;fleeing from devils and oppressors,&quot; the kindred of the </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">XXX</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> use it to refer to the necessity of turning away from one\u2019s mortal life and passing into the Requiem. It is right that mortals should not know of the vampires in their midst, and that the two societies should remain ever separate. Open interaction between the two could only result in conflict, and great suffering on both sides.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">In addition to this usage, the </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>\u1e62a\u1e25\u012b\u1e25 Ibn al-D\u016bda</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> makes it clear that kindred are to respect those mortals engaged in the pilgrimage to Mecca, and to refrain from feeding from them or impeding their progress in any way. Most leaders of the </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">XXX</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> go further than this, taking it upon themselves to protect pilgrims from other predatory denizens of the World of Darkness where they are able. The kindred who dwell in the holy city itself are particularly hostile to non-Muslim vampires for fear that they may prey on those coming to city, and maintain a constant vigil for such \u201cpoachers\u201d during </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>Hajj</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> season.</span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3209,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","user":"CatDoom","date":". 08-23-2015, 02:17 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","name":"CatDoom","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-23T01:22:18-07:00","id":636806,"title":"","content":"Love it. An excellent work.<br /> And for the name, they could be the Banu Shaitan covenant from Ancient Mysteries.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>LAND OF THE DAMNED: SPAIN (Spanish): </b><a href=\"https://goo.gl/3MHWYm\" target=\"_blank\">Land of the Damned: Spain</a>, <a href=\"https://goo.gl/52b0TX\" target=\"_blank\">Kingdoms of Blood: Spain</a>; Cities of the Damned: <a href=\"https://goo.gl/XFMSSe\" target=\"_blank\">Barcelona</a>, <a href=\"https://goo.gl/AXxdgq\" target=\"_blank\">Valencia</a>, <a href=\"https://goo.gl/zkI5a1\" target=\"_blank\">Carthian Constitution</a> (1812), <a href=\"https://goo.gl/5H8M4s\" target=\"_blank\">Three Arrows Pact</a>: <br /> <br /> <b>OTHERS (Spanish): </b><a href=\"https://goo.gl/36HngB\" target=\"_blank\">Demon: The Redemption</a>, <a href=\"https://goo.gl/6B9V8l\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Forgotten</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/247-uxas","name":"Uxas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar247_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"394"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-23T01:46:01-07:00","id":636813,"title":"","content":"Good work!, is a shame nobody did this before, but I think it lacks some vampiric &quot;flavor&quot;. The Lancea is a dark twist of catholic sacraments, It justifies tormenting the faithless and enforces to protect the good sheep . Even with their best intentions, vampires should corrupt everything in their own unique way.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3008-masa","name":"Masa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3008_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"130"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-23T02:38:15-07:00","id":636823,"title":"","content":"I like that it's quite low-key, not just a copy of the Lance with muslim magic.<br /> <br /> Do they need to be a covenant? I mean the Lance are their own thing because Christianity has traditionally had some form of seperate church hierarchy that was heavily removed from normal society, while the Circle are organised into exclusive cults because a lot of their practices are kinda anti-social.<br /> <br /> This on the otherhand looks to me like the general view point of a Muslim vampire. It doesn't say how they should be organised or what their goals should be.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-23T19:49:32-07:00","id":637646,"title":"","content":"I made a comment about this over on the rpg.net forums, but a Muslim who was embraced could potentially see themselves as having become a djinn (or possessed by one). This dovetails neatly with Requiem as djinn have their own courts and kings, analogous to covenants and typical city government. From my limited understanding, djinn are often described as tempter spirits, so that also feats neatly into the Lancea et Sanctum paradigm.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1257-kerensky","name":"Kerensky","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1257_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"72"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-23T20:31:43-07:00","id":637660,"title":"","content":"Well, this is generally supposed to be just the bare bones. I imagine that the covenant has a fair number of similarities with the L+S, though that doesn't come across all that well here. Predestination (<i>al-qadar</i>) is a fundamental part of the Muslim faith, and this belief lends itself to the idea that vampires have some kind of divine mandate that justifies what they do. They would have some kind of proprietary advantage similar to the other covenants... possibly Theban Sorcery, particularly considering that the Muslim world encompasses the region where it originated. They'd probably describe their blood sorcery it in terms of <i>B</i><i>arakah, </i>blessings or divine power, which flows from God and allows those to which it has been given to perform miracles.<br /> <br /> As for jinn, in Islam they're believed to have free will just like humans. They can be evil, but they can also be good Muslims. Some are believed to have the ability to possess people (at least in parts of the Muslim world), and Iblis was a jinn\u012b in the early history of the world who disobeyed God and subsequently became the rough equivalent of the Christian devil. He and his minions whisper to people and tempt them to evil, but that's a choice they've made rather than something that they're compelled to do. Most jinn keep to their own, invisible communities in remote areas and don't interact much with people.<br /> <br /> Incidentally, as you may have noticed, predestination and free-will are both part of Islamic thought, and are considered to be compatible with one another. It's not all that different, I don't think, from the idea in many types of Christianity that people are responsible for their own choices, but that God already knows what those choices are going to be. Naturally, there has been a fair amount of discussion of the problem of evil in Islamic philosophy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3209,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","user":"CatDoom","date":". 08-23-2015, 11:33 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","name":"CatDoom","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-24T03:33:49-07:00","id":637836,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/636699-a-muslim-covenant?p=636823#post636823\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I like that it's quite low-key, not just a copy of the Lance with muslim magic.<br /> <br /> Do they need to be a covenant? I mean the Lance are their own thing because Christianity has traditionally had some form of seperate church hierarchy that was heavily removed from normal society, while the Circle are organised into exclusive cults because a lot of their practices are kinda anti-social.<br /> <br /> This on the otherhand looks to me like the general view point of a Muslim vampire. It doesn't say how they should be organised or what their goals should be.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's my point. It seems indeed a very, very good work on how Islam can be viewed and practiced by vampires.<br /> <br /> But it doesn't seem to be a Covenant.<br /> <br /> Lancea et Sanctum is a Covenant because it mirrors the Catholic Church, and that institution have a strong hierarchy. It is a Covenant on the principle that only an initiate in the mysteries of the faith can lead the congregation, and the church as an institution need power and resources of its own. Indeed, the Lancea ISN'T the Covenant of the faithful, it is the Covenant of priesthood. All kindred are expected to attend mass, what the Lancea do is to be the body of priests and the secular Church. But those precepts are lacking in Islam. They don't have a central church, don't have the notion that you need an initiation to be privy to the secrets of Koran, don't have any reason for a Covenant based only on the precept of being Muslim.<br /> <br /> Not that it invalidates this work. You don't need Covenants to do things like this to be relevant or play a role in your chronicle. I'm going to use that material, by the way, because I DO have a Muslim character and she was wondering how to come to terms with her condition and her faith. I'll show this to the ST and see what we can do in the game.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-24T10:14:33-07:00","id":638140,"title":"","content":"I dunno; I feel like the distinction is somewhat artificial. The Lancea et Sanctum is basically built on a core of Christian beliefs (the Crucifixion and Resurrection, Sin and Hell, the Eucharist, the second coming and the final Judgement, etc.) with a number of noteworthy deviations. These deviations are justified by the Sanctified's claim that they have a unique holy book (the <i>Testament of Longinus</i>), which, among other things, provides instructions on how to be a Christian vampire. This book is nevertheless broadly compatible with, and (as far as I can tell) used alongside, the Bible; it even contains direct quotations of Bible passages.<br /> <br /> My Muslim vampire group has a unifying philosophy built on a core of Muslim beliefs (thus far described in terms of the &quot;Pillars of Islam,&quot; though obviously there's more to it than that), with a number of noteworthy deviations. These deviations are justified by their claim that they have a unique holy book (the <i>\u1e62a\u1e25\u012b\u1e25 Ibn al-D\u016bda</i>), which, among other things, provides instructions on how to be a Muslim vampire. This book is nevertheless broadly compatible with, and used alongside, the Koran, and it probably contains direct quotations or references to Koranic passages.<br /> <br /> As for the Sanctified being the priests of their religion, that's not unambiguous. Their write-up in <i>Blood and Smoke</i> makes it seem that way, but the &quot;Anointed&quot; merit explicitly states &quot;Not all Sanctified are members of the clergy. Most are lay members.&quot; These two sections aren't <i>necessarily</i> contradictory; in many forms of Christianity, laypeople can hold positions in the church hierarchy or otherwise take on specific religious roles. For instance, most monks and all nuns are laypeople, in the sense that they haven't been ordained as priests.<br /> <br /> In any event, while Islam doesn't have priests in the sense of religious specialists who interact with God on behalf of the faithful, it certainly does have <i>clergy</i>. Most of the religious titles in Islam are scholastic, rather than authoritarian, recognizing one of the faithful for their knowledge of the theory and practice of <i>ad-d\u00edn</i> (religion). However, there are, in most branches of Islam, specialists who have made a career of studying and administration of Islamic law, and there are additionally congregational leaders who regularly preach sermons and instruct the faithful on the principals of the religion. In essence, you could have a Muslim religious covenant that wasn't <i>too </i>fundamentally different from the Sanctified, who are, after all, described as often being scholars and historians, in addition to being priests. There would be considerable differences in the details of the organization, of course, but that's why you'd want a separate covenant, as opposed to just having a Muslim congregation of Sanctified.<br /> <br /> As far as hierarchy goes, it's true that the structure of a Muslim covenant would presumably be less hierarchical than most incarnations of the Lance. But we have examples of covenants that are less hierarchical, so I don't see that as a huge problem.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3209,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","user":"CatDoom","date":". 08-24-2015, 01:28 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","name":"CatDoom","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-24T10:38:13-07:00","id":638181,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CatDoom</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/636699-a-muslim-covenant?p=638140#post638140\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My Muslim vampire group have a unifying philosophy built on a core of Muslim beliefs (thus far described in terms of the &quot;Pillars of Islam,&quot; though obviously there's more to it than that), with a number of noteworthy deviations. These deviations are justified by their claim that they have a unique holy book (the <i>\u1e62a\u1e25\u012b\u1e25 Ibn al-D\u016bda</i>), which, among other things, provides instructions on how to be a Muslim vampire. This book is nevertheless broadly compatible with, and used alongside, the Koran, and it probably contains direct quotations or references to Koranic passages.</div> </div> </div> </div>Maybe it would be worth considering this group more analogous to the Invictus or Carthians than the Lancea: rather than a group founded <i>for</i> the practice of a religion, it's a group founded for establishing a social order for vampires and their interactions with humanity; but unlike the two political covenants, it does so <i>via</i> the practice of a religion. Simply put, their core philosophy could be as much that &quot;Islam provides the tools for vampires to live together and in a way that minimises the harm done to Kindred and human ummas alike&quot; as &quot;Islam is the one true religion&quot; (not that there wouldn't be significant pressure towards the latter).<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> However, there are, in most branches of Islam, specialists who have made a career of studying and administration of Islamic law, and there are additionally congregational leaders who regularly preach sermons and instruct the faithful on the principals of the religion. </div> </div> </div>And if they have a magical side to them, rather than blood sorcery, maybe the judgements of these scholars manifest in a manner similar to Carthian Law.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","name":"SunlessNick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-24T11:09:00-07:00","id":638203,"title":"","content":"I like it. You should flesh it out with some covenant roles and merits. You might draw some inspiration from Invictus Oaths and &quot;Carthian Law&quot;, so the fat\u0101w\u0101 from a Kindred mufti would be backed by supernatural force or consequences. Also, you might address the covenants views on Ghouls and relationships with mortals.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6617-vyrkolash","name":"Vyrkolash","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"60"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-24T13:39:26-07:00","id":638314,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/636699-a-muslim-covenant?p=638181#post638181\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Maybe it would be worth considering this group more analogous to the Invictus or Carthians than the Lancea: rather than a group founded <i>for</i> the practice of a religion, it's a group founded for establishing a social order for vampires and their interactions with humanity; but unlike the two political covenants, it does so <i>via</i> the practice of a religion. Simply put, their core philosophy could be as much that &quot;Islam provides the tools for vampires to live together and in a way that minimises the harm done to Kindred and human ummas alike&quot; as &quot;Islam is the one true religion&quot; (not that there wouldn't be significant pressure towards the latter).<br /> And if they have a magical side to them, rather than blood sorcery, maybe the judgements of these scholars manifest in a manner similar to Carthian Law.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I really like that, actually. &quot;Islam is the one true religion&quot; isn't much of a philosophy in places where nearly everyone already takes that for granted, much like how you probably don't have a lot of &quot;convert the heathen!&quot; type Sanctified in places that are already solidly Christian. Of course, vampires seem to cling to old, pagan beliefs longer than mortals do (perhaps unsurprisingly), but still, I think it's a good idea to emphasize the covenant as a body that tries to maintain an appropriate relationship between vampire and mortal believers. Of course, with vampires &quot;appropriate&quot; doesn't mean anything close to &quot;warm and fuzzy,&quot; and there's no reason that Muslim vampires would be any less prone to justifying self-deception than others of their kind.<br /> <br /> I also love the idea of using a system similar to Carthian Law. I've seen a number of people complain that they don't think Carthian Law fits the themes of the Movement very well, and while I'm okay with the advantage myself, I think it could probably fit and Islam-centric covenant even better. Of course, it would be easy to slip into &quot;cut off your hand if they don't like your face&quot;-type stereotypes, but with even a token effort to relate the system to actual Islamic law, you could probably come out with something much better than that.<br /> <br /> I'll see what I can cook up myself, and see about fleshing out the social and philosophical elements covenant a bit more as well... stay tuned. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","name":"CatDoom","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-24T19:24:27-07:00","id":638697,"title":"","content":"You actually didn't got what I meant.<br /> <br /> I don't think the idea is too weak for a Covenant. I think it is too strong. I don't think it is just a congregation of the Sanctified. I think the Sanctified don't even stand a chance at these lands.<br /> <br /> Why not, instead of just one Covenant, you color the Covenants of those countries with Islam? The scholastic and alchemical traditions of Islam make for a good Covenant with similarities to the Ordo Dracul, but based on Islam. The complex and refined (if sometimes rough) law system of Islam makes for a good base to anything resembling the Invictus in those countries to embrace the Koran with passion, and maybe develop something like Carthian Law instead of Oaths. Other Covenants, if present, like a Carthian-like one trying to underpower the &quot;Invictus&quot;, would either be as colored by Islam as them, or otherwise have to account for it in a strong way.<br /> <br /> So it sums up to: don't make a Muslim Covenant. Make other Covenants and make them be also Muslim. Get rid of the Lancea altogether, except as a foreign religion (it isn't impossible for those people to accept a little Christian presence).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-24T19:36:38-07:00","id":638707,"title":"","content":"I support this idea.And OP used it in the past. In Ancient Mysteries there were two Muslims covenants: <br /> - The Banu Shaitan, the most religious group, their equivalent to the Lances et Sanctum.<br /> - The al-Amin, an Invictus-like covenant center in Islamic Law doctors.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>LAND OF THE DAMNED: SPAIN (Spanish): </b><a href=\"https://goo.gl/3MHWYm\" target=\"_blank\">Land of the Damned: Spain</a>, <a href=\"https://goo.gl/52b0TX\" target=\"_blank\">Kingdoms of Blood: Spain</a>; Cities of the Damned: <a href=\"https://goo.gl/XFMSSe\" target=\"_blank\">Barcelona</a>, <a href=\"https://goo.gl/AXxdgq\" target=\"_blank\">Valencia</a>, <a href=\"https://goo.gl/zkI5a1\" target=\"_blank\">Carthian Constitution</a> (1812), <a href=\"https://goo.gl/5H8M4s\" target=\"_blank\">Three Arrows Pact</a>: <br /> <br /> <b>OTHERS (Spanish): </b><a href=\"https://goo.gl/36HngB\" target=\"_blank\">Demon: The Redemption</a>, <a href=\"https://goo.gl/6B9V8l\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Forgotten</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/247-uxas","name":"Uxas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar247_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"394"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-25T00:22:59-07:00","id":638862,"title":"","content":"Honestly, I kind of feel like it wouldn't do justice to the diversity of cultures in the world to simply take the existing covenant archetypes and add a little local flavor. I think that <i>parallels</i> are great, but there's no reason for there to always be five specific kinds of power bloc wherever there are vampires. In the case of the Muslim world, I don't really think there's a compelling reason to have an &quot;Islamic Invictus&quot; as an entirely separate group from an &quot;Islamic Lancea et Sanctum.&quot; If the al-Amin are scholars of Islamic law, what do the Banu Shaitan even <i>do</i>, really? As has been mentioned, Islam doesn't really have priests, per se, and the religious leaders they do have tend to be scholars of Islamic law, in addition to philosophy and ethics (since the three subjects are closely related, particularly in Islamic thought).<br /> <br /> There would certainly be more than one Covenant in the Muslim world. In addition to the Circle of the Crone, the Carthian Movement (who have probably tried to take advantage of the the Arab Spring to make a power play), and the Lancea et Sanctum (who are probably still holding on even in areas that have very few Christians these days), I could see there being a home-grown spin on the Ordo Dracul, particularly since there are rumors the the Mae-Ji, who founded a similar (though explicitly Buddhist) group in Thailand, disappeared after traveling west. I think that was intended to leave open the possibility that Dracula may have actually met and learned from her, but if so she would have passed through a whole lot of Muslim territory.<br /> <br /> You might also have some sort of Sufi-inspired, mystically-oriented group like the Sufis, who might practice some kind of blood magic of their own.<br /> <br /> If I may digress for a moment, I am of the opinion that the Carthian Movement and the Circle of the Crone should be among the most omnipresent of the covenants, even outside of places directly colonized by Europeans. The Circle of the Crone have always been presented, as far as I can tell, as more or less an alliance of convenience, made up of radically different religious groups united mostly by the fact that they're relatively small and opposed by larger, more monolithic religious movements. The Carthians, on the other hand, probably recruit anywhere that political ideologies like democracy and communism have taken root, to whatever degree. That includes more or less the entire world at this point, since these ideologies have been among Europe's most successful exports.<br /> <br /> Anyway, I do agree in principal that &quot;Muslim vampires&quot; is too broad a concept for a single covenant. You can be Muslim and belong to lots of different kinds of organizations. I've tried to narrow the focus of the covenant to a certain extent by placing an emphasis on interpreting Islamic law for vampires, and by giving them a specific historical origin, their own hadith, and so on, in much the same way that the history and unique dogma of the Lancea et Sanctum prevents them from simply being &quot;Christian vampires.&quot;<br /> <br /> Sorry if that came off as kind of rambling, or as defensive. I do appreciate your feedback, and I'm really tired, so it might not be the best time to be writing a response.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3209,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","user":"CatDoom","date":". 08-25-2015, 03:38 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","name":"CatDoom","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-25T04:30:31-07:00","id":638936,"title":"","content":"So. I hate to be the voice of dissent, but this feels more like &quot;how to be a Muslim while also being a vampire&quot; and less like a &quot;Vampire Covenant.&quot; This isn't really about dealing with, expressing, or refining the curse. It isn't really about defining one's requiem.<br /> <br /> The Lancea et Sanctum isn't even remotely close to actual Catholicism and is, at best, a highly blasphemous heresy built out of the very worst parts of Catholicism and then presented in a highly evocative way. If you're going to approach an Islamic covenant while maintaining the same tone of feel of the Lancea et Sanctum you have to be willing to go to the same type of place with Islam.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1157-soulgambit","name":"SoulGambit","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"912"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-25T09:34:42-07:00","id":639171,"title":"","content":"Yeah, the Lancea et Sanctum isn't just a place for religious vampires. Like all the covenants, it's sort of a focal point of the Requiem. At its most basic level it gives you something to do, a reason to go on for the rest of your potentially limitless existence, and a set of rules and ideas that keep you with Humanity.<br /> <br /> So far the covenant seems to offer a bunch of restrictions, but what does it urge vampires to do? In the Lancea et Sanctum, vampires are gods demons on Earth. They have a reason to exist, to tempt and torment the faithful and faithless. What does the <i>\u1e62a\u1e25\u012b\u1e25 Ibn al-D\u016bda</i> offer a vampire as a reason to keep on going?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-25T14:15:57-07:00","id":639461,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CatDoom</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/636699-a-muslim-covenant?p=638862#post638862\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If the al-Amin are scholars of Islamic law, what do the Banu Shaitan even <i>do</i>, really? As has been mentioned, Islam doesn't really have priests, per se, and the religious leaders they do have tend to be scholars of Islamic law, in addition to philosophy and ethics (since the three subjects are closely related, particularly in Islamic thought).</div> </div> </div> </div>According to Ancient Mysteries about the al-Amin:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ancient Mysteries</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The administrative government of the Muslim Kindred<br /> was known as al-Amin, \u201cthe faithful\u201d or \u201cthe trustworthy.\u201d<br /> The covenant, while primarily concerned with matters of<br /> governance, never divorced itself from the mortal faith of<br /> Islam to the extent of the western Invictus. Kindred of this<br /> covenant favored an exoteric interpretation of Islam, and<br /> the princes, known as Sultans or Caliph, of their domains,<br /> called Ummah, drew their laws directly from the Qur\u2019an.</div> </div> </div> </div>And about the Banu Shaitan:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ancient Mysteries</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Banu Shaitan, on the other hand, remained deeply<br /> interested in the spiritual underpinnings of Islam, mining<br /> the Qur\u2019an and other holy texts for esoteric truths<br /> to illuminate Allah\u2019s meaning in the existence of the<br /> Kindred. Like the Lancea+Sanctum, the Banu Shaitan<br /> believed that the role of the Kindred under Allah rests in<br /> protecting and testing the faithful. Unlike the Sanctified,<br /> however, the Banu Shaitan had little interest in converting<br /> other Kindred to their faith or infidel mortals to Islam.<br /> The group delved deeply into the metaphysical and<br /> mystical, and when the first exhausted Lancea+Sanctum<br /> pilgrim warriors reached the Holy Land in 1099 they<br /> were shocked to find non-believers with a command of<br /> the Dark Miracles equal to their own.</div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>LAND OF THE DAMNED: SPAIN (Spanish): </b><a href=\"https://goo.gl/3MHWYm\" target=\"_blank\">Land of the Damned: Spain</a>, <a href=\"https://goo.gl/52b0TX\" target=\"_blank\">Kingdoms of Blood: Spain</a>; Cities of the Damned: <a href=\"https://goo.gl/XFMSSe\" target=\"_blank\">Barcelona</a>, <a href=\"https://goo.gl/AXxdgq\" target=\"_blank\">Valencia</a>, <a href=\"https://goo.gl/zkI5a1\" target=\"_blank\">Carthian Constitution</a> (1812), <a href=\"https://goo.gl/5H8M4s\" target=\"_blank\">Three Arrows Pact</a>: <br /> <br /> <b>OTHERS (Spanish): </b><a href=\"https://goo.gl/36HngB\" target=\"_blank\">Demon: The Redemption</a>, <a href=\"https://goo.gl/6B9V8l\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Forgotten</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/247-uxas","name":"Uxas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar247_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"394"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-25T14:34:40-07:00","id":639468,"title":"","content":"Yeah, the al-Amin are closer in practice to Invictus in terms of Western convenants, its just that the cultural idea of secular and religious authority being different things is not a historic concept in the Islamic world (obviously it exists in many majority Muslim countries today, but vampiric society is always anachronistic). More importantly, al-Amin are are devoted directly to (un)living by the teachings of Islam (like the Ashirra in the Classic World of Darkness), while the Banu Shaitan are a subversion like Lancea+Sanctum to serve the role of &quot;holy monsters&quot;, and embrace their sins as divinely ordained.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-25T18:10:45-07:00","id":639669,"title":"","content":"Just for the record, the Lancea et Sanctum ALSO don't differentiate between secular and religious authority. The distinction between First State and Second state is precisely that applied to a religious hierarchy (something the Islam doesn't have). The Lancea craves badly for secular power, an hoards it greedily when achieves it.<br /> <br /> And yeah, they're actually a early Christian heresy that grew next to the Catholic Church.<br /> <br /> <b>CatDoom</b>, I think I understood you and, if right, we're on the same boat now.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-28T01:42:19-07:00","id":642733,"title":"","content":"After the feedback I've gotten here, I'm looking into approaching something like this from a somewhat different angle. I'm currently working on a writeup that more-or-less follows the format used to present the Covenants in <i>Blood and Smoke</i>; I'll post it here when I'm done. In the mean time, I'd love to see related homebrew from other folks here, if anyone has any.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","name":"CatDoom","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-28T02:41:33-07:00","id":642780,"title":"","content":"Maybe I make one in the weekend, if I manage enough time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-15T01:52:12-07:00","id":1140452,"title":"","content":"NOTE: I wrote this for another similar thread, I figured it would make sense to post it here, too. If you see some references to what others have said, it might be from the other thread.<br /> <br /> Hi, everybody,<br /> <br /> I am Muslim and I bumped into this thread as I was planning to narrate a chronicle set in Cordoba in the year 1000 CE, so culturally-Muslim vampires will feature heavily.<br /> <br /> Note: I only became aware of the Banu Shaytan and Iblic Creed stuff reading your posts, and I am going to check them out immediately. Thank you.<br /> <br /> First, to be clear: Islam's abrogations of previous revelations does not mean they stop being true and of divine origin. It simply means that the norms they set are no more binding and that, if they say something explicitly denied by the Quran (such as Jesus - peace be upon him - being the Son of God), that is to be considered a human addition. Beyond this, Muslims throughout history, even pious ones reputed to be saints/walis, have had all sorts of attitudes to previous religions. Also, there are some Muslims who do not believe in abrogation and see previous religions as still valid, though they are few and criticized for this. They exist, nonetheless, so Muslim-cultured Kindred might well claim similar ideas.<br /> <br /> My personal take on the Lancea et Sanctum as seen by vampires in Muslim lands is this: when Islam appeared, the Byzantine Empire was mostly Sanctified (i.e.: LeS), Arabia was Circle of the Crone and Persia (Zoroastrianism) was CotC or something else (still need to figure it out, since Persian stuff does not feature in my Cordoba chronicle).<br /> Early human converts to Islam, once embraced, would not find Muslim guides in their vampirism. The closest thing they would find were Sanctified teachers. Longinism would make more sense to them than adapting Islam to vampirism because:<br /> 1) Islam's religious precepts are evidently not appliable to vampires;<br /> 2) only God can issue religious norms, so vampires can't just take the five pillars, dietary restrictions and other rules and adapt them to vampires (like five prayers at night, don't drink blood of drunkards or pig-eaters, starve to torpor/frenzy in Ramadan, etc), as one would need divine authority for that. Regarding the prayers, I prefer not making them perform any of the five daily prayers, not even the night ones, on the basis that, drinking human blood and being more or less walking corpses, they would be considered ritually impure in various schools of Islamic law, thus being forbidden from performing the five obligatory prayers (although they can perform other forms of prayer that do not require ritual purity, such as dhikr - the remembrance of God - or dua - supplication and personal conversation with God);<br /> 3) Muslims acknowledge previous prophets, including Jesus (peace be upon him), so the tale of Longinus receiving guidance can work. Theologically, he may be classed as a wali (saint) from the community of Jesus, as opposed to a prophet in the strict Islamic theological sense, and this would make things easier;<br /> 4) surah al-Jinn (72nd surah of the Quran) says some Jinn were listening to the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) once while he was reciting the Quran and recognised its divine origin. In general, Jinn are believed to be of different kinds, to descend from Iblis/Lucifer/the Devil/Shaytan/Satan, to be responsible for part of polytheism (as jinns made humans worship them as gods), to be able to mate with humans and have offspring from them at times, to have free will, to follow different religions - the same humans follow, since, as far as I learnt, they do not get their own prophets but are guided by human prophets and saints, hence the idea of Longinus being a wali/saint instead of a vampire prophet. Muslim-cultured vampires may see themselves as a sub-species of Jinn (the Strix may well be another sub-species), especially if this is how Arabian pre-Islamic vampires saw themselves. Whichever vampire claimed to have interacted with the Prophet (peace be upon him) or his Companions and received instructions from them may have simply said that the main order was to carry on with Longinism, just knowing that Jesus is not God or His Son, etc., but without prescribed rules that differ from Longinism;<br /> 5) the Quran and the Hadith do not mention (to my knowledge) what rules Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) set for the Jinn, although it's generally understood that they did receive rules from him and previous prophets (peace be upon them all), so whatever rules Longinism imposes on vampires, that can be claimed as the rules set for them;<br /> 6) Longinism does not see Longinus as God, but only as a kind of prophet. This does not overtly contrast the Islamic creed, although it would make it slightly in theological debates easier to see Longinus as a wali/saint or sort of mystical disciple of Jesus. As a wali, he could still receive visions and inspirations, though there are some slight theological differences between walis and prophets.<br /> 7) there are various interpretations of the verses of the Quran about the crucifixion. It's enough to say that Longinian &quot;Muslim&quot; vampires stick to the view that Jesus (peace be upon him) was put on the cross, pierced by Longinus, and then taken up to heaven (before dying). <br /> <br /> In my setting, a Lancea et Sanctum Council was called in Damascus in 800 CE and established that the Church of Longinus was independent of the developments of human religions and its members were free to believe that Muhammad (peace be upon him) was a prophet or not, settling the issue for most adherents (obviously there can always be minor conflicts and divisions here and there, but they don't entail the forming of a wholly different Covenant). Moreover, vampires were not meant to push humans to one or the other Abrahamic religion, as this could effectively harm the appeal of that religion, but only remind humans of God, making them fear Him, tempt them to test them, and fight the Circle of the Crone and any vampire who promoted polytheism or atheism.<br /> <br /> I assume that there are vampires who carry on their mortal religion (whether Islam or Christianity or Judaism or other), but these usually act alone and have not formed proper Covenants that can rival with the LeS. It is quite common for new vampires, too, to hang onto one's mortal religion for a while, but this is usually temporary. One exception that I plan to include in the Chronicle is a group of vampire vampire-hunters called &quot;at-Tawwabun&quot; (the Repentant), who believe the only way to be forgiven by God is by killing other vampires and feeding only from animals forbidden to Muslims (ex.: pigs and predatory animals). This would be an antagonist covenant, however, not open to players. I have more interesting ideas about them, but I'll share them in time, God willing.<br /> <br /> In my Cordoba, the Bishop/Great Imam of the Lancea et Sanctum/ar-Rumh wal-Quds (I use double names to reflect the double culture of the vampires of Muslim Spain) has imposed on the city's vampire to feed only from sinful humans and not to kill the kine, unless they were condemned to death by the human authorities. In addition to this, Longinians are encouraged to shepherd the flock they belonged to in life (vampires who were Muslim prey on Muslims and vampires who were Christian prey on Christians), as they are most likely to understand how to remind their victims of God, but this is only an encouragement.<br /> <br /> I hope all the above makes sense. I am going to read the material about the Banu Shaytan and see how that is, God willing.<br /> <br /> I like the idea of CatDoom with the Sahih ibn al-Duda, but the idea I presented above makes more sense to me personally.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}